  "content": {
    "id": "p8-q16-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "16",
    "prompt": "By considering the expansions of both $(1+x)^n$ and $(1+x)^{2n}$, show that\n$$\\sum_{k=0}^{n}\\binom{n}{k}^{2}=\\binom{2n}{n}.$$",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "$$\\sum_{k=0}^{n}\\binom{n}{k}^{2}=\\binom{2n}{n}.$$\n\nCompare the coefficients of $x^n$ in $(1+x)^n(1+x)^n=(1+x)^{2n}$, using $\\binom{n}{n-k}=\\binom{n}{k}$.",
      "worked": "By the binomial theorem,\n$$\\begin{align*}\n(1+x)^n(1+x)^n&=(1+x)^{2n}\\\\\n(1+x)^n(1+x)^n&=\\left(\\sum_{k=0}^{n}\\binom{n}{k}x^k\\right)\\left(\\sum_{j=0}^{n}\\binom{n}{j}x^j\\right),\\\\\n(1+x)^{2n}&=\\sum_{r=0}^{2n}\\binom{2n}{r}x^r.\n\\end{align*}$$\nIn the product, a term in $x^n$ comes from multiplying a term in $x^k$ by a term in $x^{n-k}$. Its coefficient is therefore\n$$\\sum_{k=0}^{n}\\binom{n}{k}\\binom{n}{n-k}.$$\nSince $\\binom{n}{n-k}=\\binom{n}{k}$, this coefficient is $\\sum_{k=0}^{n}\\binom{n}{k}^{2}$. The coefficient of $x^n$ in $(1+x)^{2n}$ is $\\binom{2n}{n}$. Equating coefficients gives\n$$\\sum_{k=0}^{n}\\binom{n}{k}^{2}=\\binom{2n}{n}.$$",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 85
  },
